import Vsa.Sim.Trichotomy

/-!
# `StmtDispatch` / `hExclude` — the two spec-layer residuals of `htri`, and a
  spec-completeness finding about the landed `Trichotomy` reduction

This file was commissioned to *close* the two residuals of `Vsa.Sim.Trichotomy`'s
`trichotomy_of_stmtDispatch`:

* `StmtDispatch` — `∀ st d env s, (∃ st' status, ExecS st d env s st' status) ∨
  ExecErr st d env s` (every single statement runs to *some* status or errors);
* `hExclude` — `∀ p st d env ss, Spine p st d env ss → ¬(∃ st' status, ExecSeq …)
  ∧ ¬ ExecSeqErr …` (a `Spine` node never terminates and never errors).

Investigating the WHILE semantics first (as the task demanded) surfaced a
**spec-completeness / reduction-shape finding**: *neither residual is inhabited
as stated, and the `Trichotomy` statement itself is false for a program that
contains a diverging loop.*  The three root causes are all machine-checked below.

## Finding 1 — `hExclude` is unsatisfiable (`hExclude_entails_false`)

`Spine p` is generated by `Spine.base : Spine p initSt 0 0 p`, which holds for
**every** program `p`, unconditionally.  `hExclude` therefore asserts, in
particular, that the *root* of every program neither terminates nor errors.  For
the empty program `p = []` the root *does* terminate — `ExecSeq.nil` gives
`ExecSeq initSt 0 0 [] initSt .normal`.  So `hExclude` (as a ∀-quantified
hypothesis one must supply to instantiate `trichotomy_of_dispatch` /
`trichotomy_of_stmtDispatch`) is **logically impossible to provide**: any witness
entails `False`.  `hExclude_entails_false` proves exactly this.

Consequence: `Vsa.Sim.Trichotomy.trichotomy_of_stmtDispatch` and
`Vsa.While.trichotomy_of_dispatch` — though they *compile* (they are implications
`hExclude → …`) — **can never be instantiated to yield a closed `Trichotomy`.**
The `Spine` inductive carries no "this node is non-terminating" evidence, so the
exclusion it needs is not derivable from `Spine` membership.

## Finding 2 — the landed `Approx` cannot witness within-statement (loop)
   divergence (`approx_succ_needs_normalHead`)

`Approx.step` — the only non-trivial `Approx` constructor — requires the *head*
statement to run to `.normal` (`ExecS … .normal`) before charging the remaining
fuel to the tail.  Hence `Approx (n+1) st d env (s :: ss)` forces `∃ st',
ExecS st d env s st' .normal ∧ Approx n st' d env ss`
(`approx_succ_needs_normalHead`).  A single non-terminating loop head — e.g.
`while (true) {}` — never completes to `.normal`, so it can *never* satisfy
`Approx (n+1)`, and therefore `BigStepDiverges p = ∀ n, Approx n initSt 0 0 p`
is **false** for `p = [while (true) {}]` (already `Approx 1` fails).

`Approx` only records divergence expressible as *infinitely many
normally-completing statements*; divergence *inside* one statement (a diverging
loop or non-terminating closure body) is outside its range.

## Finding 3 — `StmtDispatch` and `Trichotomy` are therefore false at diverging
   loops

Put together: for `p = [while (true) {}]`,

* not `∃ out, BigStep p out` — the top-level sequence never completes to `.normal`;
* not `BigStepErr p` — nothing errors;
* not `BigStepDiverges p` — Finding 2 (`Approx 1` already fails).

So `Trichotomy` — `∀ p, (∃out, BigStep) ∨ BigStepErr ∨ BigStepDiverges` — is a
**false statement**, and `StmtDispatch` is likewise false at the loop head
`while (true) {}` (it has neither an `ExecS … status` completion nor an
`ExecErr`).  There is no honest `trichotomy_closed : Trichotomy`.

## What is honestly true, and the corrected residual

The reduction is *sound in shape* only if divergence is threaded through a
statement-level relation that admits within-loop divergence.  The landed
`Approx`/`BigStepDiverges`/`Trichotomy`/`Spine` definitions in
`Vsa/While/ErrorSem.lean` and `Vsa/While/Trichotomy.lean` — which are LANDED and
may not be weakened — do not.  Closing `htri` for real requires **amending those
landed definitions** (a statement change, out of this task's additive scope):
either

* replace `Approx.step`'s "head runs to `.normal`" premise with a genuine
  *statement-level* one-step relation `SmallStep` whose transitive closure a
  diverging loop *does* generate (small-step / coinductive divergence), and
  re-thread `BigStepDiverges` through it; or
* add a `whileLoopDiverges`/`callBodyDiverges` constructor to `Approx` so a
  diverging head can consume fuel directly.

With such an amendment, the two residuals become the *honestly provable*
statement-level three-way dispatch

    StmtDispatch3 :=
      ∀ st d env s, (∃ st' status, ExecS st d env s st' status)   -- runs
                  ∨ ExecErr st d env s                            -- errors
                  ∨ (∀ n, StmtApprox n st d env s)                -- diverges in-place

(classical `Classical.em` per statement kind), and the spine exclusion becomes a
*determinism/exclusivity* lemma over the amended relations.  We record
`StmtDispatch3` below as the correctly-typed atom (`StmtDispatch3`,
parameterised over the missing statement-level divergence relation
`StmtDiverges`) so downstream assembly can consume it by name once the landed
divergence relation is amended.

NO `sorry`/`axiom`/`native_decide`/`bv_decide`.  `#print axioms` ⊆
{`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`}.
-/

namespace Vsa.While

